            <ArticleTitle>A comparison of results from center-median and basal thalamotomies for pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>341-51</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In a personal series of 43 patients with chronic pain treated by thalamotomy, 23 patients had basal thalamotomy and 19 had center-median thalamotomy. Forty-nine procedures were performed. The best results occurred in patients with pain in the upper part of the body and in patients who had bilateral center-median thalamotomy. Bilateral center-median thalamotomy was superior to basal thalamotomy and had fewer side effects. Despite adverse reports, thalamotomy remains a useful analgesic procedure.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
